# mark: TokenStream =============================================================================

## What TokenStream<T> asks of its token type, Token or ComponentValue (filled by hand, r30a):
## the calls the C++ template body makes on a T (`is`, `to_string` through String::join's
## formatter, `to_debug_string`), and make_eof()'s `if constexpr (IsSame<T, ...>)` choice as a
## method that ignores its receiver.
pub interface TokenStreamToken[T]:
    func token_stream_is(type_: TokenType) -> bool
    func token_stream_to_string() -> ak.String
    func token_stream_to_debug_string() -> ak.String
    func token_stream_make_eof() -> T

## Web::CSS::Parser::TokenStream (Libraries/LibWeb/CSS/Parser/TokenStream.h:19): a generic struct.
pub struct TokenStream[T]:
    pub var m_tokens: const T[]
    pub var m_index: usize  # C++ default: { 0 }
    pub var m_marked_indexes: ak.Vector[usize]
    pub var m_eof: T

## Web::CSS::Parser::TokenStream::StateTransaction (Libraries/LibWeb/CSS/Parser/TokenStream.h:21),
## filled by hand (r30a). The C++ class is nested in the template, but it only reads and restores
## the stream's index, so it holds a pointer to `m_index` instead of the `TokenStream<T>&`, and is
## one type for every T. Its destructor is `state_transaction_destroy`, called explicitly (with
## `defer`) where the C++ transaction goes out of scope.
pub struct StateTransaction:
    pub var m_parent: StateTransaction*?  # C++ default: { nullptr }
    pub var m_token_stream_index: usize*  # TokenStream<T>& m_token_stream: its m_index
    pub var m_saved_index: usize  # C++ default: { 0 }
    pub var m_commit: bool  # C++ default: { false }
